evībhiḥ\nDat/Abl: devībhyaḥ\nGen: devīnām\nLoc: devīṣu\nSo dual inst/dat/abl: devībhyām; plural inst: devībhiḥ, etc.\nSo for ī-stems, the base before endings often becomes ī? Need separate.\n\nThis is getting complicated. We need accurate paradigms for both i-stem feminine and ī-stem feminine.\n\nGiven reference lumps II_STEM, but we can produce correct forms based on actual ending.\n\nOur feminine i-stem lemmas: avakḷpti, bhūmi, bhūti, buddhi, maybe cañcu? No, cañcu is u-stem. cāṅgerī is ī-stem. ceṣṭā is ā-stem.\n\nSo need patterns for i-stem (feminine) and ī-stem (feminine). Also ā-stem (AA_STEM) for ceṣṭā.\n\nLet's formulate.\n\nII_STEM (i-stem feminine, short i) paradigm using mati/buddhi:\nBase = stem minus final i (e.g., buddh)\nForms:\nN sg: buddhiḥ (base + iḥ)\nDual: buddhī (base + ī)\nPlural: buddhayaḥ (base + ayaḥ)\nAcc sg: buddhim (base + im)\nDual: buddhī (base + ī)\nPlural: buddhīḥ (base + īḥ)  // note accusative plural -īḥ\nInst sg: buddhyā (base + yā)  // buddhi + ā -> buddhyā? Actually buddhi + ā = buddhyā (i + ā -> yā)\nDual: buddhibhyām (base + ibhyām)\nPlural: buddhibhiḥ (base + ibhiḥ)\nDat sg: buddhyai (base + yai) or buddhaye? For feminine i-stems, dative singular is -yai? Let's check mati: dative singular matyai? I think both matyai and mataye are seen, but standard is matyai? Actually according to grammar, feminine i-stems have dat sg -yai, -aye? For i-stems, both are possible? In classical, matyai is more common. I'll use -yai.\nDual: buddhibhyām\nPlural: buddhibhyaḥ\nAbl sg: buddhyāḥ (base + yāḥ) or buddheḥ? Standard -yāḥ.\nDual: buddhibhyām\nPlural: buddhibhyaḥ\nGen sg: buddhyāḥ (base + yāḥ)\nDual: buddhyoḥ? Actually gen/loc dual: buddhyoḥ? For i-stem feminine, dual gen/loc is -yoḥ? Example: matyoḥ. So buddhyoḥ.\nPlural: buddhīnām (base + īnām)\nLoc sg: buddhyām (base + yām) or buddhau? Standard -yām.\nDual: buddhyoḥ\nPlural: buddhiṣu (base + iṣu)\nVoc sg: buddhe (base + e)\nDual: buddhī (base + ī)\nPlural: buddhayaḥ (base + ayaḥ)\n\nCheck: mati forms: Nom sg matiḥ, acc matim, inst matyā, dat matyai, abl matyāḥ, gen matyāḥ, loc matyām, voc mate. Dual N/A/V matī; inst/dat/abl matibhyām; gen/loc matyoḥ. Plural N/V matayaḥ; acc matīḥ; inst matibhiḥ; dat/abl matibhyaḥ; gen matīnām; loc matiṣu. Good.\n\nII_STEM (ī-stem feminine, long ī) paradigm using nadī:\nBase = stem minus final ī? For nadī, base nad. But forms sometimes add y before vowel endings.\nForms:\nN sg: nadī (base + ī)\nDual: nadyau (base + yau) // not nadī? Actually nadī -> nadyau\nPlural: nadyaḥ (base + yaḥ)\nAcc sg: nadīm (base + īm)\nDual: nadyau (base + yau)\nPlural: nadīḥ (base + īḥ) // accusative plural\nInst sg: nadyā (base + yā)\nDual: nadībhyām (base + ībhyām)\nPlural: nadībhiḥ (base + ībhiḥ)\nDat sg: nadyai (base + yai)\nDual: nadībhyām\nPlural: nadībhyaḥ\nAbl sg: nadyāḥ (base + yāḥ)\nDual: nadībhyām\nPlural: nadībhyaḥ\nGen sg: nadyāḥ (base + yāḥ)\nDual: nadyoḥ? Actually gen/loc dual: nadyoḥ? For ī-stem, gen/loc dual is nadyoḥ? I think yes: nadyoḥ (nadī + oḥ -> nadyoḥ). So base + yoḥ.\nPlural: nadīnām (base + īnām)\nLoc sg: nadyām (base + yām)\nDual: nadyoḥ\nPlural: nadīṣu (base + īṣu)\nVoc sg: nadi (base + i) // vocative singular shortens ī to i\nDual: nadyau (base + yau)\nPlural: nadyaḥ (base + yaḥ)\n\nCheck devī: Voc sg devi, dual devyau, plural devyaḥ. Good.\n\nSo for cāṅgerī, stem cāṅgerī, base cāṅger. Use above.\n\nNow AA_STEM (feminine ā-stem) like senā:\nBase = stem minus final ā (e.g., sen). Forms:\nN sg: senā (base + ā)\nDual: sene (base + e)  // not senau? For ā-stem dual nom/acc/voc is -e? Actually feminine ā-stem dual nom/acc/voc is -e? Let's recall: senā (army) declension:\nSing: N senā, A senām, I senayā, D senāyai, Ab senāyāḥ, G senāyāḥ, L senāyām, V sene\nDual: N/A/V sene? Or senau? I think sene? Wait feminine ā-stems dual nom/acc/voc is -e? Example: kanyā -> kanye? Actually standard: senā dual nom/acc/voc = sene (not senau). Yes, because ā-stem feminine dual uses -e. \nPlural: N/V senāḥ, A senāḥ? Actually accusative plural senāḥ? No, ā-stem accusative plural: senāḥ? Let's check: For ā-stem feminine, accusative plural is -āḥ? Wait, senā: plural accusative senāḥ? That seems same as nominative. I think yes: senāḥ. But sometimes -ās? Actually in Sanskrit, feminine ā-stems: Nom plural senāḥ, Acc plural senāḥ (same). So -āḥ.\nInst sg: senayā (base + ayā? Actually senā + ā -> senayā? The final ā becomes ay before vowel: senayā)\nDual: senābhyām (base + ābhyām)\nPlural: senābhiḥ (base + ābhiḥ)\nDat sg: senāyai (base + āyai)\nDual: senābhyām\nPlural: senābhyaḥ (base + ābhyaḥ)\nAbl sg: senāyāḥ (base + āyāḥ)\nDual: senābhyām\nPlural: senābhyaḥ\nGen sg: senāyāḥ (base + āyāḥ)\nDual: senayoḥ (base + ayoḥ? Actually senā + oḥ -> senayoḥ)\nPlural: senānām (base + ānām)\nLoc sg: senāyām (base + āyām)\nDual: senayoḥ\nPlural: senāsu (base + āsu)\nVoc sg: sene (base + e)\nDual: sene (base + e)\nPlural: senāḥ (base + āḥ)\n\nCheck: dhenu? Not.\n\nSo ceṣṭā: stem ceṣṭā, base ceṣṭ. Forms accordingly.\n\nNow U_STEM masculine/neuter (e.g., guru). But we have masculine u-stem: brahmabandhu (masc), cañcu (feminine but U_STEM), maybe cakṣus is US_STEM not U. Also bṛhaspati is I_STEM.\n\nU_STEM masculine paradigm (guru):\nBase = stem minus final u (e.g., gur).\nN sg: guruḥ (base + uḥ)\nDual: gurū (base + ū)\nPlural: guravaḥ (base + avaḥ)\nAcc sg: gurum (base + um)\nDual: gurū (base + ū)\nPlural: gurūn (base + ūn)\nInst sg: guruṇā (base + uṇā)  // note retroflex ṇ due to r? Actually guruṇā, because rule? For non-r stems, -unā? For guru, due to r, n becomes ṇ? Actually guru + inā = guruṇā? The ending is -inā, but after u, n becomes ṇ? Standard: guruṇā. For śatru, inst sg śatruṇā? Actually śatru + inā = śatruṇā? I think yes, because n after u or ṛ becomes ṇ? But for mṛdu, mṛdunā? Hmm. The ending is -inā, but sandhi: u + i = vi? Actually guru + inā = guruṇā? Let's derive: guru (stem) + inā (instrumental singular ending for u-stems). The combination guru + i- would normally yield gurv-inā? No, u-stem instrumental singular ending is -inā? Actually for i-stems it's -inā, for u-stems it's -unā? Wait, guru instrumental singular is guruṇā? Let's check: guru means heavy; instrumental singular is guruṇā. The ending is -nā, with stem final u? So it's guru + nā = guruṇā (with retroflex). For mṛdu, instrumental singular is mṛdunā? Actually mṛdu + nā = mṛdunā (dental n because no r? But there is r in mṛdu? The r is at beginning, so n becomes ṇ? The retroflexion rule depends on preceding r/ṛ even not immediately? It applies if stem contains r/ṛ not followed by palatal/cerebral/dental? So mṛdunā? I think it might be mṛdunā with dental n because r is not immediately before? Actually the rule: n becomes ṇ if preceded by r, ṛ, or ṝ even at a distance unless interrupted by certain letters. So mṛdu has r at start, so n would become ṇ? But many forms show mṛdunā? Let's verify: mṛdu (soft) instrumental singular is mṛdunā? I think it's mṛdunā with dental n because the n is not immediately after the retroflex? Actually the rule requires the n to be in the same word and preceded by ṛ, ṝ, r, or ṣ, and not separated by a palatal, cerebral, or dental consonant. In mṛdu, the n is separated by d (dental), so no retroflexion? The rule says not if a dental intervenes? Actually the intervening letters that block retroflexion include dentals? Yes, a dental consonant blocks the retroflexion. So mṛdunā with dental n. For guru, g-u-r-u, no intervening consonant, so r directly precedes the n? Actually guru + nā: g u r u n ā. The r is followed by u, which is not a consonant, so it allows retroflexion? The rule says if r is separated by a vowel, still applies? I think yes, so guruṇā. So guru and śatru (śatru has r? śatru contains r? śatu? Actually śatru has r? शत्रु has r? It's ś-a-t-r-u, yes r followed by u, so instrumental is śatruṇā? I think yes. So we'll use -uṇā for stems with r? But not important maybe. Standard grammar: for u-stems, instrumental singular ending is -unā, and the n is changed to ṇ if the stem contains r/ṛ not followed by a dental etc. Our stems: brahmabandhu (contains r? brahmabandhu: b-r-a-h-m-a-b-a-n-dh-u, has r in brahma, but also has ndh? Need simplify: We'll use standard ending -unā and not worry about retroflexion? But correct forms require ṇ if applicable. For brahmabandhu, the stem has r in brahma? brahmabandhu = brahma + bandhu. The r in brahma might cause retroflexion? The n in ending is after dh? There is also bandhu with n? Actually brahmabandhu has r? brahma contains r? ब्रह्म has r? It has r? ब्रह्म = b r a h m a, yes r. But there is also h? The retroflexion rule: r followed by a vowel, then h? h is a consonant? Actually in Devanagari, ब्रह्म has conjunct br + h + m? So r is followed by h? The rule may be complex. I'd rather avoid overcomplicating; many resources use dental n for such words? Actually for brahmabandhu, instrumental singular would likely be brahmabandhunā (dental n) because the r is followed by h? In traditional grammar, r followed by a vowel can cause retroflexion, but if there is an intervening h? Hmm. We'll just use the standard ending -unā with dental n for simplicity, unless we are certain. But for guru, we can use guruṇā. But our u-stems: brahmabandhu (masculine), cañcu (feminine). We'll use -unā (dental) for brahmabandhu? Might be wrong; maybe brahmabandhunā. For cañcu, no r, so cañcunā? But feminine u-stem instrumental singular is not -unā but -vā? Earlier we said feminine u-stem has inst sg dhenvā. So cañcu inst sg would be cañcvā? Actually cañcu + ā -> cañcvā? The stem cañcu + ā -> cañcvā (u + ā -> vā). So not -unā. So for feminine u-stem, use dhenu-like pattern. So we'll handle cañcu separately.\n\nThus for masculine u-stem brahmabandhu, use guru-like:\nN sg: brahmabandhuḥ\nDual: brahmabandhū\nPlural: brahmabandhavaḥ\nAcc sg: brahmabandhum\nDual: brahmabandhū\nPlural: brahmabandhūn\nInst sg: brahmabandhunā? (maybe brahmabandhunā)\nDual: brahmabandhubhyām\nPlural: brahmabandhubhiḥ\nDat sg: brahmabandhave\nDual: