EM. I'll assume neuter? Actually melāpaka is pairing, so dvaṃdvamelāpaka is a compound that could be neuter. I'll treat as neuter a-stem.\n\n\"dvār\" (root-stem, meaning door, neuter? actually dvār is feminine? In Sanskrit, dvār is feminine, meaning door. Stem dvār-. So it's a root-stem ending in -r. So ROOT_STEM. We'll generate forms accordingly: feminine root-stem. For dvār, declension: Nom sg. dvār, dvārau, dvāraḥ? Actually, feminine nouns ending in -r (like dvār, gir, etc.) decline like root-stems. Standard: sg. N. dvāḥ (from dvār + s > dvāḥ), A. dvāram, I. dvārā, D. dvāre, Ab. dvāraḥ, G. dvāraḥ, L. dvāri, V. dvāḥ. Dual: N.A.V. dvārau, I.D.Ab. dvārbhyām, G.L. dvāroḥ. Plural: N.A.V. dvāraḥ, I. dvārbhiḥ, D.Ab. dvārbhyaḥ, G. dvārām, L. dvārsu. I'll generate these.\n\n\"dvāra\" (neuter a-stem, meaning door, gate). So A_STEM neuter.\n\n\"dvaya\" (neuter a-stem, meaning pair, duplicity). So A_STEM neuter.\n\n\"dvija\" (masc a-stem), \"dvijasattama\" (masc a-stem, superlative, basically same as dvija with -sattama), \"dvijāti\" (masc i-stem? dvijāti is a compound, meaning 'twice-born', it's an i-stem adjective, masculine, ending in -i. So I_STEM masculine, like kavi).\n\nNow, the other lemmas: \n- \"dharuṇa\" (neuter a-stem? dharuṇa is an adjective meaning supporting, but also a noun. Likely neuter a-stem? Actually, dharuṇa is an adjective that can be used as a noun in any gender. But the lemma is given without specification, I'll treat it as neuter a-stem, but it could be masculine. I'll assign A_STEM and generate masculine forms? I need to pick. Usually, if not specified, default to masculine a-stem. I'll go with masculine unless known. For dharuṇa, I'll do masculine a-stem. But double-check: dharuṇa from dhṛ, often neuter meaning 'prop, support'. So neuter. I'll do neuter. But the declension of neuter a-stem is similar to masculine except nominative/accusative singular/diff plural. So I'll generate neuter: n. sg. dharuṇam, etc. I'll assume it's a neuter noun.\n- \"d